//! core/tasks — running a piece of a program more than once at a time.
//!
//! Two shapes, and they answer two questions. `parallel` runs a list of work and
//! waits for all of it. `scope` and `spawn` start work that runs *beside* the
//! code that started it, and close over it: a scope returns when its body and
//! every task spawned into it have finished.
//!
//! ## `parallel`
//!
//! `parallel(ctx, items, f)` runs `f` over every item and answers
//! the results **in the items' order**, whatever order the work finished in,
//! and hands each call the item's own index.
//!
//! That order promise is the whole reason this module has one signature rather
//! than a family of them. No test can pin a concurrent operation whose answer
//! depends on which task won: this language has no way to say "assert one of
//! these six outputs", so a result list in completion order would be untestable
//! by construction. Fixing the order costs a `[B]` the runtime fills in rather
//! than appends to, and buys a program whose answer is the same on every backend
//! and on every run.
//!
//! `f` receives the caller's whole context as its first parameter, so a task can
//! do anything its caller could — read a file, make a request, sleep — and
//! nothing its caller could not. It arrives as a *parameter* rather than by
//! capture because a lambda may not capture an effect-carrying value (SPEC
//! 10.6).
//!
//! That is `C` in the signature below and `C` again in `effect Tasks`, and it is
//! **not** `Self`. `Self` is whatever implements `Tasks` — `host.tasks`, or a
//! test's double — which grants that one effect and nothing else, so a step
//! handed it could not read a clock or allocate. The one line of this module's
//! body is `ctx.parallel(ctx, items, f)`, and the repetition is the whole point:
//! the first says who does the scheduling, the second says what the work is done
//! with.
//!
//! Every task has finished before `parallel` returns, so a task cannot outlive
//! the context that granted it. That keeps "a program that never names
//! `host.fs` cannot read a file" true of the program's lifetime and not only of
//! its call graph. `scope`, below, buys background work without giving that up:
//! the waiting moves from the call to the scope, and there is still no handle
//! to join and nothing that outlives the scope it was spawned into.
//!
//! How much of it actually runs at once is a property of the platform, and
//! deliberately not of the signature:
//!
//! | Backend | Today |
//! |---|---|
//! | JavaScript | genuinely concurrent — it starts the tasks together and awaits them together, so two that wait overlap. Two that *compute* do not: the engine has one thread |
//! | native, `--release` | genuinely **parallel** — each task runs on a carrier of its own, on a processor of its own, so two that wait overlap and two that compute overlap |
//! | native, `buri run` | sequential, in index order, on the calling carrier |
//!
//! All three answer the same list, and that is the whole of what this module
//! promises. The release backend is the one row where `parallel` buys more
//! processors rather than only overlapped waiting, and it earns the name.
//!
//! The development backend is sequential because a program it builds has one
//! Buri stack. A waiting task still holds a frame in it, so a second task could
//! not have one. That is a fact about where its frames live, not a scheduler
//! nobody has written yet, and it changes no signature, no result and no test —
//! which is the point of writing the order promise down first rather than after.
//!
//! `parallel` is `mapCtx` with an index and a scheduler, but it does not belong
//! in `core/list`, because it is not a fact about lists. Its bound carries
//! `Tasks`, which is authority: a signature that names it says the caller was
//! granted the right to run its own work concurrently, and that has to be
//! visible where a reader looks for a program's capabilities.
//!
//! ```buri
//! from "core/effect" import { Alloc, Stdout, Tasks };
//! from "core/host" import * as host;
//! from "core/io" import * as io;
//! from "core/list" import * as list;
//! from "core/str" import * as str;
//! from "core/tasks" import * as tasks;
//!
//! export fn main(): Result<(), Str> {
//!     let ctx = context {
//!         Alloc: host.alloc,
//!         Stdout: host.stdout,
//!         Tasks: host.tasks,
//!     };
//!     let labelled = tasks.parallel(ctx, ["a", "b", "c"], fn(c, i, name) => {
//!         str.format(c, "${i}:${name}")
//!     });
//!     let _ = io.println(ctx, labelled.join(ctx, " ")).ignore();
//!     .Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! prints `0:a 1:b 2:c` — the items' order, whichever task finished first.
//!
//! ## `scope` and `spawn`
//!
//! `parallel` is for work you have a list of. Background work is not that
//! shape: a socket loop, a retry, a timer that fires in five minutes. Those
//! start once and run beside everything else.
//!
//! `scope(ctx, body)` opens a place for that work and closes it. `spawn(ctx,
//! scope, f)` puts one task there. The scope returns when the body **and every
//! task spawned into it** have finished, so nothing runs after the call that
//! opened it — the same promise `parallel` makes, moved from the call to the
//! scope.
//!
//! ```buri
//! from "core/effect" import { Alloc, Clock, Stdout, Tasks };
//! from "core/host" import * as host;
//! from "core/io" import * as io;
//! from "core/tasks" import * as tasks;
//! from "core/time" import * as time;
//!
//! export fn main(): Result<(), Str> {
//!     let ctx = context {
//!         Alloc: host.alloc,
//!         Clock: host.clock,
//!         Stdout: host.stdout,
//!         Tasks: host.tasks,
//!     };
//!     let _ = tasks.scope(ctx, fn(c, here) => {
//!         let _ = tasks.spawn(c, here, fn(c2) => {
//!             let _ = time.sleepMs(c2, 5);
//!             let _ = io.println(c2, "the timer fired").ignore();
//!             ()
//!         });
//!         io.println(c, "the body is done").ignore()
//!     });
//!     .Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! **A timer is a task that sleeps.** There is no `Timer` type and no
//! `setTimeout`: `clock.sleepMillis` already waits, and a task is already the
//! thing that waits without holding up the code around it. So the five-minute
//! timer above is five lines of ordinary program, and cancelling it is
//! cancelling a task.
//!
//! **`Scope` is inert.** It holds no context, so a lambda may capture one (SPEC
//! 10.6) — which is what lets an interface hand a scope to a handler that will
//! spawn into it later. That is the whole reason it is a value and not a
//! receiver.
//!
//! **A library cannot spawn.** It exposes a `run` and the application spawns
//! it, because a scope is the application's to open. `core/net/server`'s `run`
//! is already that shape.
//!
//! **Stopping is cooperative.** There is no `cancel`, and there is deliberately
//! nothing to add one to. A task ends when its own body ends, so a loop stops
//! by finding its socket closed, or by asking an actor whether to carry on.
//! Anything else would mean unwinding a task from outside it, and an abort in
//! this language is a write to standard error and an exit (SPEC 6.9) rather
//! than something a second task can survive.
//!
//! ### When a spawned task actually runs
//!
//! The same table as `parallel`, for the same reason: the scope's *promise* is
//! that everything has finished when it returns, and the overlap is the
//! platform's business. A scope collects what was spawned and runs a round of
//! it through `parallel`, then another round for whatever those tasks spawned,
//! until nothing is waiting. So on JavaScript two spawned tasks overlap, on a
//! native `--release` build they run on carriers of their own, and under
//! `buri run` they run one after another in the order they were spawned.
//!
//! One consequence of that last column: under `buri run` a spawned task that
//! never ends starves the ones behind it, because they wait for a round that
//! never finishes. Spawn a socket loop and a timer, and both run on JavaScript
//! and under `--release`, while under `buri run` the timer never starts
//! (`design/native/DECISIONS.md`).
//!
//! A task spawned **after** its scope's body has returned — which on a page is
//! what a handler does — runs on the task that spawned it, because the scope's
//! own drain has already ended. A page is the outer scope and it closes when
//! the page does; nothing else keeps it alive, and nothing needs to
//! (`design/native/DECISIONS.md`).

from "core/alloc" import * as alloc;
from "core/effect" import { Alloc, Tasks };

/// Runs `f` over every item and answers the results in the items' order.
///
/// The `Int` `f` receives is the item's own index, so a task knows where it is
/// without the closure counting. It could not count anyway: a lambda has nowhere
/// to keep a counter.
///
/// An empty list answers an empty list and runs nothing.
export fn parallel<C: Alloc + Tasks, A, B>(
    ctx: C,
    items: [A],
    f: fn(C, Int, A) => B,
): [B] {
    ctx.parallel(ctx, items, f)
}

// --- Background work ----------------------------------------------------------

/// Where spawned tasks run, and what waits for them.
///
/// Inert data: one handle the runtime issued, and no context. A lambda may
/// capture one (SPEC 10.6), which is what lets a page hand a scope to a
/// handler that spawns into it later.
///
/// The handle stays this module's own, for `core/actor`'s reason: a scope is
/// the authority to spawn, not the number that authority is spelled with, and
/// nothing outside here could do anything with an `Int` the runtime issued.
export struct Scope(Int);

/// Opens a scope, runs `body` in it, and answers what `body` answered — once
/// every task spawned into it has finished.
///
/// The body is handed the caller's whole context and the scope, in that order.
/// Nothing spawned here outlives this call, which is `parallel`'s promise at a
/// larger grain: a task cannot outlive the context that granted it.
///
/// A body that spawns nothing is an ordinary call with an extra argument, and
/// costs one round through the runtime to find that nothing is waiting.
export fn scope<C: Alloc + Tasks, A>(ctx: C, body: fn(C, Scope) => A): A {
    let here = Scope(scopeOpen(ctx));
    let run = body;
    let answer = run(ctx, here);
    let _ = draining(ctx, here);
    let _ = resting(ctx, here);
    answer
}

/// Starts `f` in `scope`, and answers without waiting for it.
///
/// `f` is handed the caller's whole context, so a task may do anything the code
/// around it could and nothing more — `parallel`'s rule, and for the same
/// reason: a lambda may not capture an effect-carrying value (SPEC 10.6).
///
/// `core/alloc`'s `copyAcross` deep-copies the task **out of every arena**
/// before it is queued, because the runtime holds it past this call. Without
/// it a task spawned inside an `alloc.scoped` body would reach the runtime in
/// pages the scope is about to unmap. `core/actor`'s four crossings take the
/// same copy for the same reason.
///
/// A spawn that arrives while the scope's own drain is running is picked up by
/// that drain. One that arrives after it has ended — which on a page is what a
/// handler does — runs here, on the task that spawned it.
export fn spawn<C: Alloc + Tasks>(ctx: C, scope: Scope, f: fn(C) => ()): () {
    let _ = scopePush(ctx, scope.0, alloc.copyAcross([carried(f)]));
    match (scopeEnter(ctx, scope.0)) {
        false => (),
        true => {
            let _ = draining(ctx, scope);
            resting(ctx, scope)
        },
    }
}

// --- The drain ----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Two functions and a wrapper, and between them they are the whole of the
// scheduling. `draining` is a self tail call, so a scope that runs a million
// tasks uses one frame (`middle/tail_calls.rs`; design/non-goals.md has no
// loops).

/// Every task waiting in `scope`, and everything those tasks spawn, run to the
/// end.
///
/// One **round** at a time: the runtime moves what is waiting into the round
/// and answers that round's indices, `parallel` runs them, and whatever they
/// spawned is the next round. So a task spawned by a task is waited for, and
/// the overlap inside a round is `parallel`'s — carriers on a native release
/// build, one `Promise.all` on JavaScript, index order under `buri run`.
fn draining<C: Alloc + Tasks>(ctx: C, scope: Scope): () {
    let round = scopeRound(ctx, scope.0);
    match (round.isEmpty()) {
        true => (),
        false => {
            let _ = parallel(ctx, round, fn(c, i, at) => running(c, scope, at));
            draining(ctx, scope)
        },
    }
}

/// Gives the drain up, and takes it back where something arrived while it was
/// being given up.
///
/// The one race this module has: a task that spawns just as the drain is
/// ending would otherwise wait for a drain that has already stopped. So
/// `scopeLeave` answers whether anything is waiting, and this takes the drain
/// back when it is.
fn resting<C: Alloc + Tasks>(ctx: C, scope: Scope): () {
    match (scopeLeave(ctx, scope.0)) {
        false => (),
        true => {
            match (scopeEnter(ctx, scope.0)) {
                false => (),
                true => {
                    let _ = draining(ctx, scope);
                    resting(ctx, scope)
                },
            }
        },
    }
}

/// The round's task at `at`, run once.
///
/// `false` where the slot is already empty, which nothing in this module does
/// twice — the round's indices are handed out once each. Answering a `Bool`
/// rather than `()` keeps `parallel`'s result element above zero bytes.
fn running<C: Alloc + Tasks>(ctx: C, scope: Scope, at: Int): Bool {
    match (taking(ctx, scope, at)) {
        .None => false,
        .Some(held) => {
            match (held.first()) {
                .None => false,
                .Some(carrier) => {
                    let task = carrier.value;
                    let _ = task(ctx);
                    true
                },
            }
        },
    }
}

/// The round's task at `at`, **at the type it was spawned at**.
///
/// `core/actor`'s "taking a block back" rule, at this module's one site: the
/// entries are type-erased at the key, so the type argument written at the
/// call is the only thing that says what the block holds and the compiler
/// generates the release from it. A bare `scopeTaskAt` here would leave `T`
/// determined by nothing and release a block that lets go of nothing.
fn taking<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, scope: Scope, at: Int): Option<[Carried<fn(C) => ()>]> {
    scopeTaskAt(ctx, scope.0, at)
}

/// One task on its way through the runtime.
///
/// `core/actor`'s `Carried`, at this module's one crossing and for its
/// reasons. A `[T]` is two words whatever `T` is (VALUE-MODEL.md §4), so
/// nothing about the element crosses and the runtime needs no stride, no glue
/// and no descriptor to hold a task, queue it and give it back. The `Int` is
/// there to be *some* bytes: a `[T]` whose element occupies none has no block
/// at all, and `first` would answer `.None` at any index.
struct Carried<T> {
    /// Zero, always.
    pad: Int,
    /// The task itself.
    value: T,
}

/// A task, wrapped for the crossing.
fn carried<T>(value: T): Carried<T> {
    Carried { pad: 0, value: value }
}

// --- The runtime's half -------------------------------------------------------
//
// Six operations, and the two that move a task move a **whole block** — a
// one-element `[Carried<fn(C) => ()>]` — for `core/actor`'s reason: that is
// what makes them implementable against a runtime compiled once that knows no
// Buri type. `middle/monomorphize.rs`'s `GENERIC_INTRINSICS` records the
// argument, and `cli/runtime/lib.rs` §3 the ownership rule they amend.
//
// **The runtime never calls one of these tasks.** It holds the block, hands it
// back, and `running` above enters it — which is what lets this land without
// the boundary `cli/runtime/rt.rs` §2 still calls undefined: a step whose
// closure outlives the call that handed it over. The closure outlives the call
// here as a *value*, not as a C function pointer.
//
// Each one is bound by `Tasks`, for `core/list`'s reason: the authority is in
// the signature and the key is the module's. There is no second implementation
// of a queue for a test to bind, and the one decision a double would make —
// the order the tasks run in — is `parallel`'s, one level up, where
// `core/host/testing`'s `tasks()` already makes it.

/// A fresh scope, and its handle. The caller is its drain until it leaves.
fn scopeOpen<C: Tasks>(ctx: C): Int;

/// Queues one task and answers how many are now waiting. `.None` for a handle
/// naming no scope, and the block is not taken on that path.
fn scopePush<C: Tasks, T>(ctx: C, handle: Int, task: [Carried<T>]): Option<Int>;

/// Moves everything waiting into a round and answers that round's indices.
/// An empty list where nothing is waiting, which is what ends the drain.
fn scopeRound<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, handle: Int): [Int];

/// Takes the round's task at `index`, once. `.None` where it is already taken.
fn scopeTaskAt<C: Tasks, T>(ctx: C, handle: Int, index: Int): Option<[Carried<T>]>;

/// Becomes the scope's drain, where it has none. `false` where somebody else
/// already is, and that somebody will pick up what this caller queued.
fn scopeEnter<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, handle: Int): Bool;

/// Gives the drain up, and answers whether anything is waiting — which is a
/// task that arrived while the drain was ending, and has to be taken back up.
fn scopeLeave<C: Tasks>(ctx: C, handle: Int): Bool;
